::PC_U05_L01_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U05_L01_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.1
Question:
Add (2 + 3i) + (4 + i).
{
~A. 6 + 2i#This subtracts imaginary parts instead of adding them. Add real parts and imaginary parts separately.
=B. 6 + 4i#Correct. Add real parts: 2 + 4 = 6. Add imaginary parts: 3i + i = 4i.
~C. 8 + 3i#This multiplies the real parts instead of adding them. Use addition because the expression is a sum.
~D. 2 + 12i#This multiplies the imaginary coefficients. Addition keeps real and imaginary parts separate.
}
::PC_U05_L01_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U05_L01_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.1
Question:
What is the conjugate of 5 - 7i?
{
~A. -5 + 7i#This changes both signs. A conjugate changes only the sign of the imaginary part.
~B. 5 - 7i#This repeats the original number. The imaginary sign must change.
~C. -5 - 7i#This changes the real part too. The real part stays the same in a conjugate.
=D. 5 + 7i#Correct. The conjugate of a - bi is a + bi.
}
::PC_U05_L01_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U05_L01_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.1
Question:
On the complex plane, the number -3 + 2i is represented by which point?
{
=A. (-3, 2)#Correct. The real part is the x-coordinate and the imaginary coefficient is the y-coordinate.
~B. (2, -3)#This reverses the real and imaginary coordinates. Keep a + bi as (a, b).
~C. (3, -2)#This changes both signs. Use the signs shown in the complex number.
~D. (-3, -2)#This changes the sign of the imaginary coordinate.
}
::PC_U05_L01_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U05_L01_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.1
Question:
Subtract (6 + 5i) - (2 + 8i).
{
~A. 4 + 13i#This adds the imaginary parts instead of subtracting. Distribute subtraction to both parts.
~B. 8 - 3i#This adds real parts and subtracts imaginary parts. Subtract real parts too.
=C. 4 - 3i#Correct. 6 - 2 = 4 and 5i - 8i = -3i.
~D. 4 + 3i#This subtracts real parts correctly but reverses the imaginary subtraction.
}
::PC_U05_L01_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U05_L01_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.VC.1
Question:
What geometric action represents taking the conjugate of a complex number?
{
~A. Reflection across the imaginary axis#This would change the real part sign. A conjugate keeps the real part the same.
=B. Reflection across the real axis#Correct. Changing the sign of the imaginary part reflects the point across the real axis.
~C. Rotation 90 degrees counterclockwise#A conjugate is a reflection, not a 90-degree rotation.
~D. Translation right 1 unit#A conjugate changes vertical position, not a horizontal translation.
}